---

<LessonLayout title="EX1 — Sales Order Agent: Design Exercise">
  <LessonHeader
    num="EX1"
    title="Sales Order Agent — Design Exercise"
    subtitle="All 10 cookbook phases applied to a real multi-channel sales agent (chat + email)."
  />

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <p>
      A multi-channel agent (chat + email) that takes orders, checks inventory, creates quotes,
      sends emails, handles order mutations from email threads, warns on low stock, and runs reports.
      All 10 phases applied so you can see each decision being made in real context.
    </p>

    <LessonCallout variant="key" title="Why this example is interesting">
      <p>
        Email introduces unique challenges: thread awareness, idempotency (same email arriving twice),
        implicit intent ("change it to 30" assumes context from 3 days ago), and truly irreversible
        write operations. All of these expose architectural decisions you wouldn't face with simple chat.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <!-- Phase 01 -->
    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 01 — Do you actually need an agent?</h3>
    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Phase 01 question</th><th>Answer for Sales Order Agent</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Can a single LLM call solve it?</td>
            <td>No — requires checking inventory, creating quotes, sending emails, and handling mutations. Multiple tools and dynamic branches.</td>
            <td>Keep going ↓</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Are all steps known upfront, fixed, and predictable?</td>
            <td>No — can't know whether the customer will request a quote, confirm it, modify an existing order, or just ask for status.</td>
            <td>Keep going ↓</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Dynamic decision-making, tool use, unknown steps?</td>
            <td>Yes — every conversation follows a different path depending on customer intent and what the tools return.</td>
            <td><strong>Agent justified.</strong></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <!-- Phase 02 -->
    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 02 — Define the goal and the problem</h3>
    <div class="phase-def">
      <div class="pd-row"><span class="pd-label">Goal</span><span class="pd-val">Resolve routine customer order queries — status, quotes, placements, modifications, cancellations — without sales rep involvement, across both chat and email channels.</span></div>
      <div class="pd-row"><span class="pd-label">Success metric</span><span class="pd-val">Email response time ≤ 2 min p95 (down from ~4 hours) · Zero duplicate-action incidents · Cost &lt; $0.05/interaction · Rep time on routine orders reduced by ~60%</span></div>
      <div class="pd-row"><span class="pd-label">Input</span><span class="pd-val">Chat message (string) <em>or</em> parsed email (sender, subject, body, thread_id, message_id)</span></div>
      <div class="pd-row"><span class="pd-label">Output</span><span class="pd-val">Customer-facing text reply + zero or more side effects (quote created, order placed, email sent)</span></div>
      <div class="pd-row"><span class="pd-label">"Done" means</span><span class="pd-val">Customer intent is resolved and confirmed — OR clarification was requested — OR the task was explicitly deferred to a human agent</span></div>
      <div class="pd-row"><span class="pd-label">Hard constraints</span><span class="pd-val">Chat ≤ 5 s · Email ≤ 2 min · Cost &lt; $0.05/interaction · PII never in logs · Forbidden: modify/cancel/send without interrupt()</span></div>
      <div class="pd-row"><span class="pd-label">Acceptable failure</span><span class="pd-val">Partial completion with explanation. After 3 rounds of unresolved ambiguity, escalate to a human agent — do not loop forever.</span></div>
    </div>

    <!-- Phase 03 -->
    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 03 — Choose your autonomy level: autonomy per action</h3>
    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>Autonomy level</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Conversation &amp; clarification</td><td>Autonomous</td><td>LLM decides how to ask and respond</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Intent classification</td><td>LLM decides (conditional edge)</td><td>Structured output enforces valid intents</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Read tools (inventory, order status, email thread)</td><td>Fully automatic</td><td>Read-only, unambiguous results — no decision needed</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>search_product_catalog</code> — ambiguous results</td><td>Agent asks customer to confirm</td><td>Multiple plausible matches → customer must choose. Agent never assumes.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>create_quote</code> (draft)</td><td>Automatic</td><td>Reversible — draft has no external effect</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>modify_order</code></td><td><strong>Human approval</strong></td><td>Irreversible. <code>interrupt()</code> shows diff before applying</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>cancel_order</code></td><td><strong>Human approval</strong></td><td>Irreversible. Always confirm name + qty</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>send_email</code></td><td><strong>Human approval</strong></td><td>Cannot unsend</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>create_order</code></td><td><strong>Human approval</strong></td><td>Commits inventory and billing</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <!-- Phase 04 -->
    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 04 — Design the tools: tool inventory</h3>
    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Type</th><th>Reversible?</th><th>Guard</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>check_inventory(sku, qty)</code></td><td>Read</td><td>—</td><td>None — safe to call freely</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>get_order_status(order_id)</code></td><td>Read</td><td>—</td><td>None</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>lookup_customer(customer_id)</code></td><td>Read</td><td>—</td><td>None</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>search_product_catalog(query)</code></td><td>Read · RAG</td><td>—</td><td>Vector similarity search — agent extracts descriptor first. Present candidates when ambiguous — never assume a match</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>get_email_thread(thread_id)</code></td><td>Read</td><td>—</td><td>None</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>check_email_processed(message_id)</code></td><td>Read</td><td>—</td><td>Idempotency guard — call first, every time</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>get_recent_orders(customer_id, days)</code></td><td>Read</td><td>—</td><td>Cross-thread lookup — call when orderId is null in a new email thread</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>create_quote(items)</code></td><td>Write</td><td>Yes (draft, no external effect)</td><td>Automatic — no interrupt needed</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>create_order(quote_id)</code></td><td>Write</td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td><code>interrupt()</code> required</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>modify_order(order_id, changes)</code></td><td>Write</td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td><code>interrupt()</code> with before/after diff</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>cancel_order(order_id)</code></td><td>Write</td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td><code>interrupt()</code> required</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>send_email(to, subject, body)</code></td><td>Write</td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td><code>interrupt()</code> required</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>12 tools total. Per Phase 06, these are split across two specialists so no single agent exceeds the 3–5 tool guideline. <code>get_recent_orders</code> is Email Specialist only.</p>

    <LessonCallout variant="tip" title="search_product_catalog is a RAG tool">
      <p>
        <strong>Why RAG, not MCP Resource or keyword search?</strong> Apply the data access
        decision framework (CB Phase 04 / L17):
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>MCP Resource</strong> would work only if the catalog is small enough to fit in the LLM's context window on every call. A product catalog with thousands of SKUs is too large — you'd blow the context budget just to find one match.</li>
        <li><strong>Keyword / SQL search</strong> fails the moment the customer's description diverges from catalog naming: <em>"the big blue storage thing"</em> will not match <code>LIKE '%blue%'</code> unless the description is literally in the product name.</li>
        <li><strong>RAG Tool</strong> is the right pattern: embed the query at call time, do a vector similarity search against the pre-indexed catalog, return the top-N semantically closest products. The agent only ever sees the relevant slice.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The agent's job before calling the tool is to extract the meaningful descriptor from
        the customer's message — not pass the raw sentence verbatim. The system prompt
        (Phase 07) defines exactly what to do with the results: one clear match → proceed;
        multiple plausible matches → present the list and ask the customer to choose. Never assume.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 04 — Design the tools: tool-to-agent mapping</h3>
    <p>
      With the tool inventory done, mark which node or agent uses each one. At this point in
      the process the agents aren't formally named yet — that happens in Phase 06 — but Phase 02
      already revealed the natural split: chat input and email input have different processing
      requirements, so they become two distinct specialists. Use those rough groupings here.
      Any tool that appears in more than one column is shared.
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Tool</th>
            <th>Lookup node</th>
            <th>Chat Specialist</th>
            <th>Email Specialist</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>check_inventory</code></td><td></td><td>✓</td><td>✓</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>get_order_status</code></td><td></td><td>✓</td><td>✓</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>search_product_catalog</code></td><td></td><td>✓</td><td>✓</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>get_recent_orders</code></td><td>✓</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>lookup_customer</code></td><td></td><td>✓</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>create_quote</code></td><td></td><td>✓</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>create_order</code></td><td></td><td>✓</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>check_email_processed</code></td><td></td><td></td><td>✓</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>get_email_thread</code></td><td></td><td></td><td>✓</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>modify_order</code></td><td></td><td></td><td>✓</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>cancel_order</code></td><td></td><td></td><td>✓</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>send_email</code></td><td></td><td></td><td>✓</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      The first three rows each have two ticks — <code>check_inventory</code>,
      <code>get_order_status</code>, and <code>search_product_catalog</code> are needed by both
      Chat Specialist and Email Specialist. Everything else belongs to exactly one node.
      The shared set is the MCP server.
    </p>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 04 — Design the tools: MCP server decision</h3>
    <p>
      The three shared tools move into a <code>sales-tools-server</code> that both specialists
      connect to — implement once, no duplication. The product catalog, pricing rules, and
      shipping zones are static reference data refreshed nightly — same content regardless of
      the live conversation — so they become MCP Resources. The order confirmation message is
      sent by both specialists; a Prompt Template with a <code>channel</code> parameter means
      the wording lives in one place and can be updated without touching either agent.
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Primitive</th><th>What is exposed</th><th>Why this primitive, not the others?</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Tools</strong></td>
            <td><code>check_inventory</code>, <code>get_order_status</code>, <code>search_product_catalog</code></td>
            <td>Per-call computation — the SKU or order ID comes from the live conversation and changes every time</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Resources</strong></td>
            <td><code>product://catalog</code>, <code>config://pricing-rules</code>, <code>config://shipping-zones</code></td>
            <td>Same content on every call — precomputable, refreshed on a schedule, not tied to agent state</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Prompt Templates</strong></td>
            <td><code>order-confirmation</code>, <code>low-stock-warning</code></td>
            <td>Both specialists send the same message type; one template with a <code>channel</code> arg adapts output for chat vs email</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Not in this server</strong></td>
            <td><code>modify_order</code>, <code>cancel_order</code>, <code>send_email</code></td>
            <td>Irreversible writes needing <code>interrupt()</code> — Email Specialist only, not shared, no MCP benefit</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <LessonCallout variant="tip" title="Why both product://catalog (Resource) and search_product_catalog (Tool)?">
      <p>
        These look similar but serve completely different purposes — applying the data access
        framework to each makes the distinction clear:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong><code>product://catalog</code> (MCP Resource)</strong> — the full product list,
          refreshed nightly. Used by offline processes (building or re-indexing the vector store)
          and by any agent that needs to enumerate all products (e.g. generating a full quote template).
          Same content on every call → Resource is the right primitive.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>search_product_catalog(query)</code> (RAG Tool)</strong> — runtime semantic
          search. The agent calls this when a customer describes a product in their own words.
          The tool embeds the query against the pre-built vector index and returns the top-N
          semantically closest matches. The agent only ever sees the relevant slice of the catalog,
          not the full document. Query changes every call → Tool is the right primitive.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If the product catalog were small enough to fit in context on every call, you could skip
        the RAG Tool and let the agent read the Resource directly. For a catalog with thousands
        of SKUs that won't fit, you need both: the Resource feeds the offline indexing pipeline;
        the Tool handles runtime retrieval.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="sales-tools-server.ts" code={salesMcpServerCode} />

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 04 — Design the tools: MCP client capabilities</h3>
    <p>
      Defining the server is only half of the MCP contract. The Sales Order Agent also connects
      as an MCP <em>client</em> and must declare what it provides back to the server.
      Two decisions to make explicitly:
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Client capability</th><th>Decision</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Roots</strong></td>
            <td>Not applicable</td>
            <td>Roots is a filesystem access scope — it matters for local stdio servers (code analysers, file processors) that need to know which directories they can touch. <code>sales-tools-server</code> connects over HTTP+SSE, calls APIs and databases, and never touches the filesystem. There is nothing to scope.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Sampling</strong></td>
            <td>Do not declare</td>
            <td>The server has no reason to initiate LLM calls back into the agent. Not declaring sampling means the server cannot request it — the capability is simply absent.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="sales-agent-mcp-client.ts" code={mcpClientCapabilitiesCode} />

    <!-- Phase 05 -->
    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 05 — Design state &amp; memory: state schema</h3>

    <p>
      The key challenge is emails that reference multiple orders: <em>"cancel #123 and change qty on #456 to 30."</em>
      A single <code>pendingChanges</code> field cannot hold both. The schema uses a
      <code>pendingOperations</code> array with a merge reducer so multiple operations accumulate
      without overwriting each other, plus a <code>crossThreadContext</code> field for when a new
      email references an order from a previous thread — the agent must not guess which order is meant.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="sales-agent-state.ts" code={stateCode} />

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 05 — Design state &amp; memory: intent decomposition &amp; cross-thread lookup</h3>

    <p>
      Because the state now holds an array of operations, a dedicated <strong>decomposition node</strong>
      must run before the main agent loop for email channel messages. It extracts all distinct
      operations in one cheap Haiku call, then a <strong>lookup node</strong> resolves any
      <code>orderId: null</code> entries — either from an explicit ID in the email body, or
      by surfacing recent orders for the customer to confirm.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="decompose-email.ts" code={decompositionCode} />

    <!-- Phase 06 -->
    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 06 — Single agent or multi-agent?</h3>
    <p>
      A single agent fails two of the three split criteria: the 12-tool list exceeds the 3–5
      guideline, and one system prompt handling both chat conversation and email idempotency +
      multi-operation decomposition would have directly conflicting responsibilities.
      Decision: <strong>supervisor + 2 specialists + 2 pre-processing nodes.</strong>
    </p>
    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Agent?</th><th>Responsibility</th><th>Model / tools</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Supervisor</strong></td>
            <td>No — routing function</td>
            <td>Reads <code>state.channel</code>, returns a route key. No reasoning needed — a plain conditional edge.</td>
            <td>No LLM · No tools</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Decomposition node</strong> <em>(email only)</em></td>
            <td>Yes — LLM agent</td>
            <td>Reads the full email thread, extracts every distinct customer intent into <code>pendingOperations[]</code> as structured JSON.</td>
            <td>Haiku · No tools</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Lookup node</strong> <em>(email only)</em></td>
            <td>Yes — LLM agent</td>
            <td>For each <code>orderId: null</code> operation: reasons from email context clues, calls <code>get_recent_orders</code>, resolves where confident. Leaves unresolvable ones null for the specialist to surface to the customer.</td>
            <td>Haiku · <code>get_recent_orders</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Chat Specialist</strong></td>
            <td>Yes — LLM agent</td>
            <td>Real-time conversation: inventory checks, product search, quote creation, order placement, order status.</td>
            <td>Sonnet · <code>check_inventory</code>, <code>get_order_status</code>, <code>search_product_catalog</code>, <code>create_quote</code>, <code>create_order</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Email Specialist</strong></td>
            <td>Yes — LLM agent</td>
            <td>Processes <code>pendingOperations[]</code> sequentially: resolve remaining nulls → load state → build diff → <code>interrupt()</code> → execute on approval.</td>
            <td>Sonnet · <code>get_order_status</code>, <code>check_inventory</code>, <code>search_product_catalog</code>, <code>modify_order</code>, <code>cancel_order</code>, <code>send_email</code></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>5 nodes total. 4 are LLM agents with their own system prompts. The Supervisor is the exception — it is a routing function, not an agent. It never calls an LLM.</p>

    <!-- Workflow map for Sales Order Agent -->
    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 06b — Sketch the workflow map</h3>
    <p>
      Before writing a single line of prompt or code, draw the graph. Below is the
      complete topology for the Sales Order Agent — two top-level paths (chat vs. email),
      each with their own pattern and parallelism strategy.
    </p>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">System-level routing</h3>
    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart TD
  IN([Inbound\nrequest]):::e --> SUP[Supervisor\nrouter]:::dec
  SUP -->|channel = chat| CHAT[Chat Specialist]:::s
  SUP -->|channel = email| IDEM[Idempotency\ncheck]:::s

  CHAT --> CHAT_END([reply to\nuser]):::d

  IDEM -->|already processed| SKIP([skip]):::w
  IDEM -->|new| DEC[Decomposition\nnode]:::s
  DEC --> LK[Lookup\nnode]:::s
  LK --> MAIL[Email Specialist]:::s
  MAIL --> OPS{more ops?}:::dec
  OPS -->|yes| INTR([interrupt /\nawait approval]):::w
  INTR -->|approved| MAIL
  INTR -->|rejected| STOP([stop chain]):::w
  OPS -->|no| DONE([send reply\nemail]):::d

  classDef e   fill:#1e3a8a,color:#fff,stroke:#1e3a8a,stroke-width:2px
  classDef dec fill:#ede9fe,color:#4c1d95,stroke:#7c3aed,stroke-width:2px
  classDef s   fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
