    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Pattern rationale — why each component chose its pattern</h3>
    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Pattern chosen</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Supervisor → Chat / Email</strong></td>
            <td>Branching</td>
            <td>Input channel determines the entire processing path. A router function reads <code>state.channel</code> and returns a route key — classic conditional edge.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Email pipeline</strong> (idempotency → decompose → lookup → specialist)</td>
            <td>Linear</td>
            <td>Steps are ordered and each depends on the previous result. No branching needed between them — only a single path through pre-processing.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Multi-operation processing</strong> (op[0] → interrupt → op[1] → ...)</td>
            <td>Cyclic (sequential)</td>
            <td>Each operation must be approved before the next starts. Operations are <em>dependent</em> — op[0] rejection stops the chain. Send API / Promise.all() would be wrong here because tasks are not independent.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Inventory lookups for a quote</strong> (multiple SKUs)</td>
            <td>Fan-out / Promise.all()</td>
            <td>Each SKU check is independent. No ordering constraint. Promise.all() over <code>check_inventory</code> calls is the lightest-weight parallelism option — no extra nodes needed.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Chat Specialist agent loop</strong></td>
            <td>Cyclic (conversational)</td>
            <td>The agent loops until the user's need is resolved or <code>max_turns</code> is hit. A cyclic pattern with a quality/completion check before looping back.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

