    <!-- Phase 08 -->
    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 08 — Add safety &amp; guardrails: guardrail plan</h3>
    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What it catches for this agent</th><th>Applied to</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>L1 — Input validation</strong></td>
            <td>Email: message_id present, body not empty, sender is a known customer ID. Chat: input not empty, under 4000 chars.</td>
            <td>Every request, before any token is spent</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>L2 — Injection detection</strong></td>
            <td>Pattern match for "ignore previous instructions", "you are now", "forget" — on <em>both</em> chat input and parsed email body/subject. Email body is untrusted input.</td>
            <td>Chat input + email body + email subject</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>L3 — PII / content filter</strong></td>
            <td>Regex for card numbers, SSNs, phone numbers in email body. Redact before the LLM processes them.</td>
            <td>Email body only (chat rarely contains card numbers)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>L4 — LLM safety classifier</strong></td>
            <td>Semantic intent check for adversarial orders ("send invoice to <em>attacker@evil.com</em>") that look valid but aren't. Run only when L1–L3 pass.</td>
            <td>Escalated inputs only (~500ms cost justified)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Idempotency guard</strong></td>
            <td><code>check_email_processed(message_id)</code> runs BEFORE any LLM call for the email channel. If already processed, return immediately without touching the LLM.</td>
            <td>Email channel only</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>interrupt() guards</strong></td>
            <td>All 4 irreversible write tools (create_order, modify_order, cancel_order, send_email) require explicit human approval before execution.</td>
            <td>All write operations</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <LessonCallout variant="warn" title="Email body is untrusted input">
      <p>
        L2 injection detection must run on the parsed email content — not just chat input.
        A crafted email subject line or body can contain adversarial instructions aimed at
        your agent. Treat it exactly like user input.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <!-- Phase 09 -->
    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 09 — Error handling &amp; resilience: email-specific failure modes</h3>
    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Failure</th><th>Response</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Duplicate email (same message_id arrives twice)</td>
            <td><code>check_email_processed</code> returns true. Reply: "This request was already processed on [date]. Result: [summary]."</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>"My order" referenced but no order found in thread</td>
            <td>Reply asking for order number. Do NOT create a new order. Do NOT guess.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>"Change qty to 30" but two active orders exist</td>
            <td>Reply listing both orders, asking which to modify. Wait for clarification before <code>interrupt()</code>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Mutation on already-shipped order</td>
            <td>Reply: "Order #4521 has already shipped and cannot be modified."</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Email body unparseable</td>
            <td>Mark as processed with 'parse_failed'. Reply asking to resend. Do NOT guess intent.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>max_turns hit mid-conversation</td>
            <td>Return the best partial result with a list of what was and was not completed. Never silently return nothing.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

