    <!-- ─── Staff-level design review ───────────────────────────────── -->
    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Staff-level design review</h2>
    <p>
      The 10 phases answer <em>how to build</em> this agent. These are the questions a staff
      engineer asks in the design review — after the phases are complete, before sign-off.
      Each is answered for the Sales Order Agent.
    </p>

    <div class="sl-intro">
      <strong>Junior engineer:</strong> "Does this work correctly?" ·
      <strong>Senior engineer:</strong> "What are the failure modes?" ·
      <strong>Staff engineer:</strong> "Who owns the consequences, and what breaks when the context changes?"
    </div>

    <!-- 1. Problem Validity -->
    <div class="sl-category">
      <div class="sl-cat-header">
        <span class="sl-badge">Problem Validity</span>
        <span class="sl-when">Before Phase 01 — is this the right intervention?</span>
      </div>

      <div class="sl-qa">
        <div class="sl-q">What does the non-AI version of this look like, and exactly why is it failing?</div>
        <div class="sl-a">Six inside-sales reps handle routine email orders manually — checking inventory in the ERP, creating a quote, replying. It fails for three specific reasons: (1) reps are a bottleneck for queries that need no human judgment, adding 4–24h latency, (2) email mutations arrive with no idempotency check, causing ~12 duplicate-action incidents per month, (3) international customers hit timezone gaps. The process itself is sound — it just doesn't scale. We are automating a working process, not a broken one.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="sl-qa">
        <div class="sl-q">If this agent works perfectly, what changes for the business in 90 days — and how do we measure it?</div>
        <div class="sl-a">Three committed outcomes: (1) routine order response time drops from avg 4 h to under 5 min, (2) duplicate-action incidents drop from 12/month to 0, (3) rep time on routine orders reduces by ~60%, measured by rep-logged time. If none of these move at the 90-day review, we have a delivery problem, not a capability problem. We agreed these metrics with the VP of Sales Operations before Phase 01.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="sl-qa">
        <div class="sl-q">What's the cost of doing nothing?</div>
        <div class="sl-a">The sales team has a headcount request for 2 additional reps to handle volume growth — ~$200K/year loaded cost. If the agent handles 60% of routine volume, that hire is no longer needed. The urgency is real: headcount approval is in the next planning cycle. Delay past that decision point means we fund headcount instead.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 2. Autonomy Contract -->
    <div class="sl-category">
      <div class="sl-cat-header">
        <span class="sl-badge">Autonomy Contract</span>
        <span class="sl-when">Phase 03 — who agreed to what, and in writing</span>
      </div>

      <div class="sl-qa">
        <div class="sl-q">Who drew the line between what the agent decides and what a human decides — and with what authority?</div>
        <div class="sl-a">The autonomy table in Phase 03 was reviewed and signed off by the VP of Sales Operations and reviewed by Legal. Legal's specific concern was <code>create_order</code> — it commits inventory and triggers downstream billing. They required <code>interrupt()</code> for all four irreversible write tools. This is documented in ADR-0042. No engineer made this call unilaterally.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="sl-qa">
        <div class="sl-q">If <code>interrupt()</code> is never triggered in 30 days of production, what does that tell us?</div>
        <div class="sl-a">One of three things, and we need to distinguish them: (1) customers are mostly asking read queries — expected, ~70% of interactions should be read-only, (2) the agent is failing before reaching write operations — check error rate and turn-completion rate, (3) the agent is bypassing <code>interrupt()</code> — this is a P1 bug. We track "interrupt() invocations as % of write-intent queries" as an explicit metric. Anything below 95% on that metric triggers an immediate investigation.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="sl-qa">
        <div class="sl-q">Who is accountable if the agent approves an action a human would have rejected?</div>
        <div class="sl-a">Named owner: Head of Sales Operations. She owns the autonomy policy and signed off that human-approval-via-interrupt() is sufficient control. If the agent causes a customer harm, it escalates to her team first, then to Legal. This is in the RACI. The accountability chain is named and documented before launch — not discovered after an incident.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 3. Failure Imagination -->
    <div class="sl-category">
      <div class="sl-cat-header">
        <span class="sl-badge">Failure Imagination</span>
        <span class="sl-when">Phases 08–09 — P99 thinking, not P50</span>
      </div>

      <div class="sl-qa">
        <div class="sl-q">What's the worst plausible thing this agent does that we haven't explicitly defended against?</div>
        <div class="sl-a">The scenario we lost sleep over: a customer submits a forged email containing another customer's order ID. <code>get_order_status(orderId)</code> would succeed and the agent would begin processing a mutation on someone else's order. The defense must be at the tool layer, not the agent layer: every <code>get_order_status</code> and <code>modify_order</code> call at the ERP level validates that <code>orderId</code> belongs to the authenticated <code>customer_id</code>. The agent trusting its own state is not enough — the external system must enforce ownership.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="sl-qa">
        <div class="sl-q">What happens when two customers simultaneously submit orders that touch the same inventory record?</div>
        <div class="sl-a">Both <code>check_inventory</code> calls return the same available qty. Both proceed toward <code>create_order</code>. We would oversell. <code>check_inventory</code> is read-only — it does not lock. The inventory reservation happens inside <code>create_order</code> at the ERP layer, which uses optimistic locking. If the ERP returns a conflict on the second call, <code>create_order</code> returns an error string. The agent informs the customer stock is no longer available and does not retry automatically. The customer confirms before the agent tries again.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="sl-qa">
        <div class="sl-q">Which assumption in the state schema becomes false when the business changes?</div>
        <div class="sl-a">The <code>channel: 'chat' | 'email'</code> union. The product roadmap has WhatsApp and Slack in the next two quarters. When those arrive, the channel field, the Email Specialist's idempotency logic, and all thread-parsing tools need to expand. We've documented this in ADR-0043 and designed the channel field as an explicit union type — not a string — so adding a new channel requires a deliberate type change rather than silently passing through.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 4. Operational Ownership -->
    <div class="sl-category">
      <div class="sl-cat-header">
        <span class="sl-badge">Operational Ownership</span>
        <span class="sl-when">Phase 10 — the 2 a.m. test</span>
      </div>

      <div class="sl-qa">
        <div class="sl-q">Who is on call at 2 a.m., and do they have everything they need — before they've ever seen this agent fail?</div>
        <div class="sl-a">Three engineers from the agent platform team, rotating weekly. Before launch they have: (1) a single dashboard — queue depth, error rate, cost/run, interrupt() invocation rate, (2) a runbook with step-by-step diagnosis for each alert type, (3) a kill switch script that routes all traffic to a static "maintenance" response in under 2 minutes, (4) full LangSmith trace access for any run_id. We are running a mandatory gameday simulation one week before launch — no one goes on call for this agent without having practiced the failure scenarios.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="sl-qa">
        <div class="sl-q">A new engineer joins in 6 months and gets paged at midnight. Walk through exactly what they experience.</div>
        <div class="sl-a">PagerDuty: "Error rate >5% on email-agent for 10 min." They open the dashboard, see the spike started at 23:47. Clicking the spike opens LangSmith filtered to failing runs. All failures show the same error: <code>check_email_processed: ConnectionError</code>. That's the idempotency Redis check. They open the runbook, section 4.2: "Redis failure — enable feature flag <code>EMAIL_IDEMPOTENCY_FALLBACK</code> to switch to Postgres." They flip the flag in the config dashboard. Error rate drops to 0 within 60 seconds. They file a P2 ticket, add a note to the incident log, and page the Redis on-call for root cause. Total time to mitigation: under 10 minutes, without any prior knowledge of this agent.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="sl-qa">
        <div class="sl-q">If the original team moves to a different project in a year, what must exist before they hand this off?</div>
        <div class="sl-a">Four non-negotiable artifacts: (1) Architecture Decision Records for every major design choice (ADR-0041 through ADR-0045), (2) the runbook — one page per alert type, tested in a gameday, (3) the golden dataset from L30 — the test suite that proves the agent behaves correctly, runnable by anyone, (4) a named business owner with the autonomy policy documented. Without these four, the next team cannot safely operate or evolve the agent — they can only fear it.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 5. Data & Trust -->
    <div class="sl-category">
      <div class="sl-cat-header">
        <span class="sl-badge">Data &amp; Trust Boundaries</span>
        <span class="sl-when">Phases 04–05 — what the agent believes vs. what it should verify</span>
      </div>

      <div class="sl-qa">
        <div class="sl-q">What does the agent believe that it has not independently verified?</div>
        <div class="sl-a">The most dangerous: the agent believes the <code>customer_id</code> from the session is the correct owner of any orderId it processes. If the authentication layer has a bug and assigns the wrong <code>customer_id</code>, the agent would process mutations on a stranger's orders. The session token must be validated at the tool boundary — <code>get_order_status</code> and <code>modify_order</code> at the ERP layer must verify ownership independently, not delegate that check to the agent's state. Defense in depth means the tool verifies, not just the agent.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="sl-qa">
        <div class="sl-q">What does the agent do when an external API returns subtly wrong data — not an obvious error, just wrong values?</div>
        <div class="sl-a">If <code>get_order_status</code> returns qty 3000 instead of 300 (an ERP data entry error), the agent will include that number in the <code>interrupt()</code> diff shown to the human approver. The human is the last line of defense against upstream data errors — this is the second reason <code>interrupt()</code> is non-negotiable, beyond agent autonomy. If we ever remove the human approval gate, we need to replace it with an automated sanity-check layer on tool outputs.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 6. Economics -->
    <div class="sl-category">
      <div class="sl-cat-header">
        <span class="sl-badge">Economics at Scale</span>
        <span class="sl-when">Phase 10 — cost curves and incident economics</span>
      </div>

      <div class="sl-qa">
        <div class="sl-q">What does this cost at 10× current volume, and is the curve linear or does it cliff somewhere?</div>
        <div class="sl-a">Current: 500 email interactions/day × $0.04 = $20/day = $7.3K/year. At 10×: $73K/year — still well below the 2-headcount alternative ($200K/year). The cost curve is linear because the architecture is stateless workers (L34) — no component costs superlinearly with volume. The only non-linear risk is Redis memory for the idempotency store, which we've capacity-planned for 100× volume. There are no cost cliffs in this design.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="sl-qa">
        <div class="sl-q">What is the cost of a P1 incident — and does the prevention cost justify itself?</div>
        <div class="sl-a">The single highest-risk P1: agent executes a write operation without <code>interrupt()</code> approval. Estimated cost: 4 engineer-hours ($800) + potential customer credit ($50–500) + legal review if PII was involved ($5–50K). Prevention cost: the <code>interrupt()</code> gate adds ~1–2s latency per write operation and ~30 lines of code per agent. The prevention cost is paid once. The incident cost is paid every time, plus the reputational cost of a customer's order being modified without consent. This math made <code>interrupt()</code> non-negotiable.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 7. Platform vs. Product -->
    <div class="sl-category">
      <div class="sl-cat-header">
        <span class="sl-badge">Platform Thinking</span>
        <span class="sl-when">Phase 06 — first of one, or first of ten?</span>
      </div>

      <div class="sl-qa">
        <div class="sl-q">Is this the first of one agent, or the first of ten — and does the answer change the architecture?</div>
        <div class="sl-a">First of ten. The sales team has a pricing agent and a returns agent in the pipeline. Before the second agent starts, three components must be extracted into a shared <code>@acme/agent-platform</code> library: the email idempotency pattern, the multi-operation decomposition node, and the <code>interrupt()</code> guard. If we don't, three teams will re-implement idempotency with three slightly different bugs, three separate runbooks, and three independent on-call rotations. This decision was made at the platform review before Sprint 1 of the Sales Order Agent.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="sl-qa">
        <div class="sl-q">Which decisions we're making today will we regret not having centralized?</div>
        <div class="sl-a">Two: (1) prompt versioning — each agent team is currently managing prompt versions in their own repo. When we have ten agents and need to audit "what prompt was running at 14:32 on March 3rd?", we'll want a central prompt registry. We're accepting this debt deliberately and have a ticket to build it before agent 3. (2) Observability schema — each agent emitting slightly different structured log fields means we can't build a cross-agent dashboard. We've agreed on a shared log schema now, even though only one agent exists.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 8. Organizational Impact -->
    <div class="sl-category">
      <div class="sl-cat-header">
        <span class="sl-badge">Organizational Impact</span>
        <span class="sl-when">All phases — the human layer that the architecture ignores</span>
      </div>

      <div class="sl-qa">
        <div class="sl-q">Which people currently do this work, and have they been part of the design — or are they finding out at launch?</div>
        <div class="sl-a">Six inside-sales reps. Two of them joined the Phase 02 problem definition session. The framing agreed with Sales leadership before design started: the agent handles mechanical queries so reps can focus on relationship-driven sales. Reps are not being cut — they are being redeployed. This was communicated to the team in the first week of the project, not the week of launch. Getting buy-in from the people whose workflow changes is as important as getting the architecture right.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="sl-qa">
        <div class="sl-q">What's the escalation path when the agent fails mid-task and a human needs to take over?</div>
        <div class="sl-a">The agent sets <code>alreadyProcessed = true</code> on the email before executing any write operation. If it fails mid-task, the customer replies to the thread. The email specialist detects <code>alreadyProcessed = true</code> but checks <code>actionsTaken</code> to see what completed. The system prompt instructs: reply with what was completed, what remains, and ask the customer how to proceed. If the customer replies with frustration signals, the CRM flags the thread for a rep to pick up. The human taking over sees a complete history — they do not start from scratch.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="sl-qa">
        <div class="sl-q">In 18 months, when the model this agent runs on is deprecated, what does migration look like and who owns it?</div>
        <div class="sl-a">Model pinning (checklist item p7) means we will get advance notice of deprecation. The migration owner is the agent platform team, not the Sales team. The process: (1) run the full golden dataset against the new model, (2) identify any behavioral regressions, (3) update system prompt as needed to restore baseline quality, (4) A/B test 5% of traffic on the new model for 2 weeks before full cutover. This process is documented now, while the knowledge is fresh. In 18 months, the engineers who built this agent may be on different teams.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
  <LessonMindMap content={mindMap} />

